# Lambda Logger Setup & Best Practices
## Overview

Lambda observability requires structured logging, distributed tracing, and metrics. AWS Lambda Powertools provides a unified approach for TypeScript. Powertools 2.31+ adds event handler tracer middleware for HTTP routes and improved Parameters error handling.

## Dependencies

```bash
pnpm add @aws-lambda-powertools/logger @aws-lambda-powertools/tracer @aws-lambda-powertools/metrics
```

## Logger Setup

### Configuration

```typescript
// src/shared/logger/index.ts

import { Logger } from "@aws-lambda-powertools/logger";

export const logger = new Logger({
  serviceName: process.env.SERVICE_NAME ?? "real-estate-api",
  logLevel: (process.env.LOG_LEVEL as "DEBUG" | "INFO" | "WARN" | "ERROR") ?? "INFO",
  persistentLogAttributes: {
    environment: process.env.ENVIRONMENT ?? "development",
    version: process.env.VERSION ?? "unknown",
    // Multi-tenant real estate: set tenantId per request via logger.appendKeys({ tenantId })
    ...(process.env.TENANT_ID && { tenantId: process.env.TENANT_ID }),
  },
});

// Re-export for convenience
export type { Logger } from "@aws-lambda-powertools/logger";
```

### Usage in Handlers

```typescript
// src/functions/create-listing/handler.ts

import type { APIGatewayProxyHandler } from "aws-lambda";
import { logger } from "@/shared/logger";

export const handler: APIGatewayProxyHandler = async (event, context) => {
  // Add Lambda context to all logs
  logger.addContext(context);

  // Add request-specific context (tenantId from auth for multi-tenant real estate)
  logger.appendKeys({
    correlationId: event.headers["x-correlation-id"],
    path: event.path,
    method: event.httpMethod,
    ...(event.requestContext?.authorizer?.claims?.sub && {
      tenantId: event.requestContext.authorizer.claims["custom:tenantId"],
    }),
  });

  logger.info("Processing create listing request");

  try {
    const body = JSON.parse(event.body ?? "{}");

    logger.debug({ body }, "Request body parsed");

    const result = await listingService.create(body);

    if (!result.success) {
      logger.warn({ error: result.error }, "Listing creation failed");
      return { statusCode: 400, body: JSON.stringify(result.error) };
    }

    logger.info({ listingId: result.data.id }, "Listing created successfully");

    return { statusCode: 201, body: JSON.stringify(result.data) };
  } catch (error) {
    logger.error({ err: error }, "Unexpected error in create listing handler");
    throw error;
  }
};
```

## Middy Logger Middleware

```typescript
// src/shared/middleware/index.ts

import middy from "@middy/core";
import { injectLambdaContext } from "@aws-lambda-powertools/logger/middleware";
import { captureLambdaHandler } from "@aws-lambda-powertools/tracer/middleware";
import { logMetrics } from "@aws-lambda-powertools/metrics/middleware";
import { logger } from "@/shared/logger";
import { tracer } from "@/shared/tracer";
import { metrics } from "@/shared/metrics";

export function createHandler<TEvent, TResult>(
  handler: (event: TEvent, context: Context) => Promise<TResult>
) {
  return middy(handler)
    .use(injectLambdaContext(logger, { clearState: true }))
    .use(captureLambdaHandler(tracer))
    .use(logMetrics(metrics, { captureColdStartMetric: true }));
}
```


## Structured Logging Best Practices

### Log Levels

| Level | Use Case | Example |
|-------|----------|---------|
| DEBUG | Development diagnostics | Function entry, variable values |
| INFO | Normal operations | Request received, operation completed |
| WARN | Unexpected but recoverable | Retry triggered, deprecated usage |
| ERROR | Failures requiring attention | Exception caught, operation failed |

### Logging Patterns

```typescript
// GOOD: Structured with context
logger.info({
  propertyId: property.id,
  listingId: listing.id,
  price: listing.price,
  action: "listing_created",
}, "Listing created successfully");

// BAD: String interpolation
logger.info(`Created listing ${listingId} for property ${propertyId}`);

// GOOD: Error with context
logger.error({
  err: error,
  propertyId,
  operation: "createListing",
  attemptNumber: retryCount,
}, "Failed to create listing");

// BAD: Error message only
logger.error(`Error: ${error.message}`);

// GOOD: Use child logger for request scope
const requestLogger = logger.createChild({
  persistentLogAttributes: {
    correlationId,
    userId: user?.id,
    requestPath: event.path,
  },
});

requestLogger.info("Processing request");
requestLogger.debug({ body: event.body }, "Request body");
requestLogger.info({ result: "success" }, "Request completed");
```


## Sensitive Data Handling

```typescript
// NEVER log sensitive data
logger.info({
  email: user.email,
  password: user.password, // ❌ NEVER
  apiKey: apiKey,         // ❌ NEVER
});

// Redact or mask sensitive data
function maskEmail(email: string): string {
  const [local, domain] = email.split("@");
  return `${local[0]}***@${domain}`;
}

function maskCard(cardNumber: string): string {
  return `****-****-****-${cardNumber.slice(-4)}`;
}

logger.info({
  email: maskEmail(user.email),
  cardNumber: maskCard(payment.cardNumber),
});
```


## Conventions

- Use AWS Lambda Powertools for unified logging, tracing, metrics
- ALWAYS include correlation ID in ALL logs (every log statement)
- ALWAYS add correlation ID as X-Ray annotation (searchable)
- Use structured logging with named properties
- Log at appropriate levels (not everything as INFO)
- Include context: operation name, entity IDs, timing
- Propagate correlation ID across ALL service boundaries (SQS, SNS, HTTP)
- Propagate X-Ray trace header (`X-Amzn-Trace-Id`) to external services
- Capture AWS SDK calls in X-Ray traces using `tracer.captureAWSv3Client()`
- Store correlation ID in database records for later tracing
- Use AsyncLocalStorage for request-scoped context
- Add X-Ray annotations for: correlationId, userId, entityId, error, statusCode
- Add X-Ray metadata for: request/response bodies, query params, debug info
- Close subsegments in finally blocks (prevent leaks)
- Return correlation ID in response headers

## Anti-Patterns

- ❌ String interpolation in log messages
- ❌ Logging sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII)
- ❌ Missing correlation IDs in logs
- ❌ Missing correlation ID propagation to downstream services
- ❌ console.log in production code
- ❌ Overly verbose logging (performance impact)
- ❌ Not capturing errors in traces
- ❌ Missing metrics for critical operations
- ❌ Inconsistent log structure across services
- ❌ Not closing X-Ray subsegments (memory leak)
- ❌ Using metadata for searchable fields (use annotations instead)
- ❌ Missing trace propagation to external HTTP calls
- ❌ Generating new correlation ID for each service (breaks tracing)

